//? Title: Forward and inverse dynamics for curiosity
//? Lang: en

> Forward and inverse dynamics for curiosity

A single encoder maps current and successor states into feature vectors.
The inverse head predicts the action from both vectors, encouraging the
encoder to retain information about controllable changes. The forward
head uses the current features and action to predict successor features.
Its regression target is detached from the gradient. The prediction
error is available as an intrinsic reward.

The example fits four directional transitions from an interior grid cell.
Two models start at the same parameters. One minimizes inverse loss alone,
and the other includes forward prediction loss with weight 0.2. The plot
tracks inverse loss and the table shows how forward prediction responds.
The learned encoder is also available directly for episodic novelty.

[Pathak et al. (2017)|https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/pathak17a.html]

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/curiosity_model.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;
  const auto rows=n::from_vector<int>({24,0,17,24,1,31,24,2,23,24,3,25},{4,3});
  const auto labels=n::from_vector<int>({0,1,2,3});
  const auto successors=n::from_vector<int>({17,31,23,25});
  std::array models{dm::rl::IntrinsicCuriosity(49,4,12,24,0.),
                    dm::rl::IntrinsicCuriosity(49,4,12,24,.2)};
  std::array<std::vector<double>,2> history;
  std::vector<double> steps(101);
  std::iota(steps.begin(),steps.end(),0.);
  std::print("| Forward weight | Inverse loss before | Inverse loss after | Forward error before | Forward error after |\n"
             "|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<models.size();++i) {
    auto& model=models[i];
    const double before=n::mean(model.evaluate(rows)[0]).item();
    for (std::size_t step=0;step<steps.size();++step) {
      const auto outputs=model.evaluate(rows);
      history[i].push_back(n::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(outputs[3],labels).item());
      if (step+1<steps.size()) model.train(rows);
    }
    const auto final=model.evaluate(rows);
    const double after=n::mean(final[0]).item();
    if (!(history[i].back()<history[i].front()) ||
        final[2].to_vector<float>()!=model.embed(successors).to_vector<float>() ||
        (i==1 && !(after<before))) {
      throw std::runtime_error("Curiosity model fit check failed");
    }
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",
               i*.2,history[i].front(),history[i].back(),before,after);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(steps,history[0],"Inverse only",{217,96,26})
      .lines(steps,history[1],"Forward and inverse",{37,99,235})
      .title("Learning action-predictive features").x_label("Adam updates").y_label("Inverse cross-entropy")
      .size(820,460).save_svg("build/curiosity_model.svg");
}
```
